WEBVTT

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.100
ARPITHA DHANAPATHI:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.900
My name is Arpitha Dhanapathi,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.210
and I'm a Principal
Product Manager

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.310
in Microsoft Purview
organization.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:12.060
Today, we're going to talk
about how you, as developers,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.580
can safeguard the data
across all of your apps

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.530
and agents' development cycle
using Microsoft Purview.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:20.580
Let's get started.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.440
Let's start by
setting the stage.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.590
There's a real and growing
anxiety around generative AI

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.340
in the enterprise and
the data it backs up.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.430
Firstly, around 55% of
leaders say that leakage

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.940
of sensitive data is their
top concern with AI.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.630
That's more than half the room
worrying about their data ending

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.470
up somewhere it shouldn't, and
it's not just hypothetical.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.440
As AI apps and agents interact
with more enterprise data,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.480
the attack surface grows.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.770
At the same time, 82% of our
leaders expect to deploy agents

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.810
in the next 12 to 18 months,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.800
so their option is not slowing
down, it's accelerating.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.930
Most organizations don't
have the visibility

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.030
or controls to keep up.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.410
Eighty percent of
the leaders say

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.390
that they lack a
clear understanding

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.520
of how AI regulations will play
out across their industries.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.300
The good news?

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.550
Organizations are taking action.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.130
Seventy-three percent
are already investing

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.240
in AI-specific data security
and compliance controls.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.470
The demand is there, and that's
exactly where Purview comes in.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.980
The question now becomes how do
you move forward as developers

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.650
with AI confidently
without putting your data

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.080
or compliance posture at risk?

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.740
Let's look at how the specific
data security concerns map

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.350
to the developer challenges
in this context.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.380
What are the concerns?

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.040
The range of data security
concerns across your apps needs

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.700
to include potential leakage
of sensitive data knowingly

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.290
or unknowingly by your users,
data oversharing concerns,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.810
as well as AI
solutions being used

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.170
in a non-compliant
fashion by your users.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.330
On the other hand, while
you as developers,

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.820
some of the decision makers
in the developer land,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.760
believe that they can handle
some of these issues,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.770
they also worry that the work

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.980
that they would be doing is
not a comprehensive solution

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.050
to address broader
security challenges

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.820
across the enterprise.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.550
Many of the developers have
acknowledged that the need

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.910
to compromise on security versus
feature development is real,

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.740
owing to the constraints
and resources.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.270
How do you address
these challenges

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.160
to unblock the
adoption of AI apps?

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.370
That brings us into the
land of Microsoft Purview.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.830
For those who are familiar
with and not familiar with it,

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.600
Microsoft Purview
enables data security

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.140
and compliance via enabling
discovery of AI risks,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.510
protection of sensitive
data against leaks,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.400
oversharing insider risks,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.550
and supporting
governance of AI usage.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.830
We'd done something
very peculiar

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.780
when the age of AI started.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.600
We collaborated with
a number of FSI,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.400
Financial Services Industries,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.600
customers to deploy M365
Copilot, the very first Copilot

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.160
that Microsoft came out with, in
highly regulated environments.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.740
We worked with these FSI
customers, and we identified

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.070
and implemented a minimum
standard for AI security

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:18.840
and AI controls that we
refer to as "FSI min-bar."

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.720
Implementing this FSI min-bar
unblocked the adoption

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:27.240
of M365 Copilot across
these large FSI companies,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.230
so now what happened?

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.560
The top customers we
have came to expect

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.970
that this min-bar is a
gold standard DSPM for AI,

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.340
Data Security Posture
Management for AI,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.780
whether it is Microsoft-built
AI or your custom-built AI.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.660
Now, what does trust
by default mean

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.150
and what role do you
as developers play?

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.770
Let's talk about how
this is relevant to you

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.900
as a developer, right?

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.090
Purview is a layer of
trust that can sit

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.370
within the development
stack in the AI.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.060
With Purview as a foundational
layer, developers need to write

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.120
up or worry about data
governance, security,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.430
and compliance themselves.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.370
They do not need to worry about
any of these aspects because all

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.220
of these will be baked in as
part of your Purview integration

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.180
within your app development
cycle, so what does that do?

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.700
Data classification
happens automatically.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.920
Access control is policy driven.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.680
Audit login is always on,
and that's what we mean

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.690
by integrated features.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:28.890
These are not add-ons on your
configuration post-launching the

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.100
apps and agents, so in terms
of the developer experience,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.560
this means simplicity.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.980
Teams can stay focused on
building great AI solutions.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.840
They don't need to
become security experts

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.710
or compliance specialists,
especially the developers.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.390
Purview handles
governance centrally

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.490
so developers can do
what they do the best,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.830
which is shipping
great products,

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.920
and this isn't just
per-app security.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.630
This is enterprise level.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.440
Think about it for a second:
one policy framework

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.770
that spans every AI
application and agent

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.050
that the organization
builds and uses.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.470
That means accelerated
development cycles

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.260
because you're not reinventing
governance for each project.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.620
It means stronger collaboration
between development teams

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.960
and security teams
because they're working

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.020
from the same playbook, and it
means security that scales.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.560
As your AI ambitions grow,
Purview grows with you.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.290
Let me show how this
works in practice.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.660
There are two paths for
you into trusted AI,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.340
and Purview meets
you where you are

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.260
with respect to these two parts.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.710
On one side, we have
the Microsoft built AI

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.320
or Microsoft platforms on
which you can build AI.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.830
If you're building on RStack,
Foundry, or Copilot Studio,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.160
you get a built-in experience
with minimal overhead.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.260
Governance is centralized.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.200
Compliance controls
are already wired up.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.410
You are essentially getting
trust as part of the platform.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.530
This is the fastest path
to production-ready AI

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:01.740
because the integration
work is already done by us.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.910
On the other hand, if you have
third-party AI and agents

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.940
that you're building, maybe
you're using open-source models,

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.160
maybe you're building with
a different framework,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.420
or maybe you're building
a multi-cloud AI

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.760
application architecture.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.680
Or, in fact, you're
probably integrating AI

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.610
into an existing application
that has its own architecture.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.430
Purview supports all of
these customer scenarios

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.920
with flexible integration
points and the ability

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.530
to extend governance
into workflows

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.620
that live outside the
Microsoft ecosystem.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.920
You don't have to choose
between flexibility and trust.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.990
Whether you are all-in
on our platform

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.700
or running a hybrid environment,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.030
Purview gives you a single
Governance layer across both.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:49.560
Now, let's walk through these
Purview-enabled AI scenarios

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.440
and what it means for security
teams in your organizations

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.960
or the enterprise customers.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.370
Once we do that, we can then
get back to looking at how you

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:02.040
as developers can help
achieve these outcomes

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.730
for your customers, starting
with AI observability.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.760
Now I'm in the Purview portal.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.270
Let's see how the data security
teams, your customers' DS teams,

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.450
today experience controls
to safeguard AI apps

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.840
and agents used in
their environments.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:27.060
As you can see here, within
Purview portal, in DSPM,

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.450
Data Security Posture Management
and AI observability,

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.120
teams can look at the inventory
of AI apps and agents

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:39.020
and corresponding
insights, right?

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:42.870
Next, a data security
admin can see all

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:46.200
of the historic activities
related to an app or agent

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.270
and drill down further
to investigate

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.540
within the activity
explorer of DSPM.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.390
For example, if there
is a DLP rule match,

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.800
Data Loss Prevention rule match,

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.070
or what sensitive data is being
exchanged in interactions,

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.170
all of this information
will be captured as part

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:04.890
of the activity explorer
as the admins dive deep

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.710
into understanding the
details of the apps or agents

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.190
from AI observability.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:17.820
Next, within the reports,
admins can get into reports

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.110
to see a high-level view

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.850
of sensitive data
that's being shared

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.400
across different AI
systems being used

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.380
within their environment,

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:28.380
what unethical interactions are
happening with these systems,

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.110
and if there are any risky
users interacting with AI

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.740
and how that is happening?

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:36.710
All of this being put together
is what we call as DSPM,

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.720
or Data Security
Posture Management,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.190
and that's one pane of Purview.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.740
Next, getting into the multiple
solutions within Purview

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:46.970
that bring the real value

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:50.420
of AI coming together,
we're looking at IRM.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.700
What you're seeing on the
screen is, within IRM,

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:54.170
there's a list of
all the agents.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.810
IRM is the Insider Risk
Management solution

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:58.230
within Purview.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.640
IRM can track and list risks for
user-driven activity as well

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:03.990
as agent-driven activity.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.700
Up until now, it was
user-driven activity,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.140
and we have recently
introduced, also,

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.150
the agents-driven activity.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.910
Admins can get a
view of all of --

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.610
when an agent triggers
a policy match,

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.460
and IRM generates the alert
and provides a full picture

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.220
to the security team, just
like how it used to do

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.490
for a user-triggered
policy match,

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:26.230
user-risk-triggered
policy match.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.510
Let's drill into what
such an alert would look

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.340
like for an IRM admin.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.080
In this alert
detail, you can see

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.490
that specific agent involved,
a user who has triggered

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:42.390
that interaction, the risk
severity, and, critically,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:46.260
the sequence of activities
that led to this alert.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.410
That includes what data
the agent accessed,

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:53.220
whether sensitive content was
present in prompts or responses,

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.550
and whether any DLP
policies were matched.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.440
All of this context
coming together

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.590
in a single pane helps
the data security teams

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:06.690
to assess whether this is a real
threat or a false positive.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.700
From there, they can
take the right action

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.520
without any guesswork.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.850
Moving on, this is our
Compliance Manager solution

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.170
again within Purview.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.530
So within the Compliance Manager
solution, the eval results

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.950
that you are seeing, evaluation
results from Azure AI Foundry,

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:26.070
are automatically
synced into Purview

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.890
for the associated agents,
and eval test status

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.480
of the agents are
updated as shown.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:36.840
Moving on to the Communication
Compliance solution,

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.670
within Communication
Compliance, as you can see,

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:41.760
the data security admin can
view all of the details

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.690
of the interactions that
the users are carrying out.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.580
They can follow up
appropriately and ensure

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.130
that the folks
interacting with AI

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:54.300
within their organization meet
the compliance requirements.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:58.260
With that, we've looked
at all the existing value

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.150
that Purview offers for AI.

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:04.170
Let me talk about something
very interesting and exciting.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.750
We are introducing
Realtime, or inline,

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.720
Data Loss Prevention
for Foundry.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:11.580
What does that mean?

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.370
For an end user, AI built

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.360
with Microsoft Foundry supports
Purview enterprise-grade data

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.360
security and compliance
with the toggle of a button

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.640
within the Foundry
control plane.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.080
As part of the integration
between Foundry and Purview,

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:31.110
we are now introducing
support for inline,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.710
or Realtime Data Loss
Prevention for Prompts.

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.270
What does this mean?

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:41.160
Security administrators can
set up a DLP policy in Foundry

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.460
within Purview to prevent
end users interacting

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.250
with Foundry-built AI
from passing any sort

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:50.670
of sensitive information
into the LLMs.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:54.000
As an admin, if I set up
a DLP policy for Foundry

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.350
in my Purview portal, I
can trigger the policy

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:02.940
when an end user passes or
interacts with the LLMs

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:04.440
with any sort of
sensitive information,

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.560
whether it is out-of-box sets
that are part of Purview

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.950
or custom-defined sets
defined by the organization.

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.680
As Purview policy
kicks in Realtime,

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.650
that particular prompt
is blocked from entering

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:20.040
into the LLM or AI, thus
preventing AI from learning

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.870
on the sensitive information

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.430
that belongs to
the organization.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:27.900
How do you achieve this?

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:30.090
Let's look at that.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.490
Within the Foundry control
plane, like I said,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:36.610
we have already introduced a
Purview integration toggle.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:42.390
Once this particular toggle
is enabled, Purview admins

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.780
in the Purview portal can start
setting up the DLP policies

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:50.580
with respect to inline DLP for
prompts, but that's not it.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:54.480
Once the toggle is enabled on
the Foundry control plane,

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.970
we're also starting to
surface the insights.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:00.870
Foundry admins can now see
relevant Foundry-specific

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:03.780
high-level insights that
are coming from Purview

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.370
for that particular
subscription.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:09.180
For all the AI apps and agents
built within that subscription,

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.430
a Foundry admin, because
of enabling the toggle,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.820
gets to see what that
posture looks like.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.010
This information is all non-PII.

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:19.470
A Foundry admin,
because they may

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.900
or may not necessarily be
security admins, they do not get

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:24.210
to see any sort of
PII-related information

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:28.170
but at least get a glimpse or a
bird's eye view of the insights

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:31.020
that they should worry
about or care about.

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:33.300
So these non-PII
insights are meant

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.310
to help Foundry admins
get a sneak peek

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.400
of the data security posture
in that subscription

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:39.540
and help connect the loop

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.930
between security
and Foundry admins.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:46.680
Moving on, what I just showed
you on Foundry is a toggle

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:50.910
of a button that integrates
Foundry and Purview,

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:52.920
but there are multiple
scenarios, of course,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:56.340
that you may not necessarily
be building on top of Foundry,

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.880
if you are building AI
apps and agents outside

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.290
of the Microsoft ecosystem,

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.420
let's look at how you
can make these apps

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:05.640
and agents Purview-aware.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:09.150
Here, I'm showing an example
of a Gen-AI app architecture

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:13.410
that uses RAG with custom
LLMs and vector DVs,

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.750
all of the architecture that
you can think of, right?

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.460
Keep in mind this framework.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:20.820
You can add any number of
elements to this architecture,

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:24.510
but the most important thing is
you can integrate with Purview

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:28.380
for your app and agent, either
via APIs or through the SDK

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:30.120
that we're going to talk about.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.850
By introducing the APIs
into your app code,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.980
you can enable the app
to be Purview-supported

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.450
and enable these apps to support
all of the value of Purview

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.040
that we just walked through.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.260
In case you don't
want to use the APIs,

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.120
there is also an SDK option.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:47.610
Purview SDK is now
directly integrated

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.100
into the Microsoft
Agent Framework SDK,

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:52.110
and what this means is

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.630
that developers can
embed Purview compliance

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:59.010
and data protection controls
right into their agent runtime.

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.410
The same enterprise-grade
governance

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.200
that protects
Copilots now extends

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:05.730
to any custom agent
that you build.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:08.820
Here's the key distinction on
how you get these capabilities.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:11.340
If you are building AI on
Foundry or Copilot Studio,

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:15.690
like I said, these integrations
are supported out of the box,

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:17.490
the toggle that I showed you.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.770
For pro developers
building custom AI

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:23.460
with Agent Framework SDK,
whether with Langchain, OpenAI,

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:26.760
Semantic Kernel, or
any other framework,

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:29.250
you can add the Purview
policy middleware

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.620
to your agent's
workflow pipeline.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:34.140
That intercepts
prompts and responses,

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:35.850
interacting with the agents,

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:40.050
and also enforces whatever
policies your tenant security

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.540
admins have
configured in Purview

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.570
for those agents
and applications.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:50.490
A few lines of code
that relate to Purview,

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:54.630
and your agent inherits the
full enterprise trust layer.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:57.480
We have some samples on
GitHub in C#, in Python,

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:01.260
and Node.js to get you started,
and let me show you what

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.490
that looks like in practice.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:11.100
The few lines of code that I've
copied here, 15 to 20 lines

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:15.210
at max, if you are building an
agent using Agent Framework SDK,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:19.530
adding these few lines of code
will introduce Purview SDK

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:23.940
as a middleware within
your agent's code base,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.960
and that starts supporting
the entire Purview value

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.450
that we just walked through
in this session until now.

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.730
Just these few lines of code,

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:36.300
the Purview server starts
getting triggered.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.120
Prompts and responses are
going to be passed to Purview.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:42.330
Policy Setup in Purview starts
acting on these agents,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.100
and all of this
action is taken care

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:46.770
of with these few lines of code.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.320
Switching gears a little
bit for now, we have looked

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:53.250
at what cloud agents do and
that all the applications

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:57.750
that you're building on cloud
can get support from Purview --

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:00.540
Foundry, Purview SDK, et cetera.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:04.910
At the same time, increasingly,
agents are running locally:

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:08.570
coding assistance in IDEs,
automation on laptops,

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:10.670
tools on endpoints your
organization does not

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:12.110
fully control.

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:15.380
These can access files,
credentials, and source code

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.790
that never touch your cloud.

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.400
Your existing governance
does not reach them.

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.320
That's the gap.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:25.500
The agent landscape is now
a mix of cloud and local,

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.630
managed and unmanaged.

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:30.210
Our commitment is that no matter
where agents are running,

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.240
where agents live, built,
deployed, or discovered,

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.790
Purview delivers the same
visibility, protection

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:39.690
and risk control, the same
framework extended everywhere.

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:40.890
Let me show you how.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:44.310
Introducing Data Security
for Local Agents,

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:45.750
and what does that mean?

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.930
The same pillars that we
look at for DSPM for AI

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:51.600
in Cloud applies the same here,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.010
and the pillars
include Discover,

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.230
Protect, and Investigate.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:57.420
It all comes down to
these three pillars.

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:59.160
These map directly
to the life cycle

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:02.490
of how you manage risk
around local agents.

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:05.070
Let's start with Discover.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:08.040
The first challenge with local
agents is simply knowing

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.210
they exist.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:11.340
When agents are running
on developer machines

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.610
or endpoints outside your cloud
environment, you need a way

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:17.460
to inventory them,
understand what they are,

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:21.900
what data they're accessing, and
what risk they might introduce.

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:25.350
DSPM gives you that view of
every agent in your environment,

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:28.920
even the ones your security
team didn't know about.

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.810
Next is Protect, and this is

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:33.000
where it gets
really interesting.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.280
We are bringing data
loss prevention

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.380
to the local agent layer.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.570
Think about what a
local agent can access

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:42.960
on a developer's machine:
source code, credentials,

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:46.770
customer data, internal
documents and whatnot.

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:49.770
DLP ensures that sensitive
data does not leak

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:52.560
through agent interactions,
whether that's through prompts,

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:54.780
responses, or tool calls.

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.090
The same DLP policies
you already use

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:00.600
for cloud agents now
extends to local ones.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.100
No separate configuration,

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:06.800
no gaps in coverage,
how cool is that?

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:08.600
Finally, Investigate.

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:10.160
You need the ability
to investigate

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.240
when something looks off.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.210
Maybe an agent is behaving
in unexpected ways,

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:19.230
or a user is trying to use a
local agent to bypass controls.

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:22.830
Insider risk management gives
you the signals and the context

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:25.650
to detect those
patterns, correlate them

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:29.460
with user behavior, and take
action before a small issue

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.680
becomes a serious incident.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:33.780
At Build, we are introducing
these capabilities

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:37.560
for managed agents with Claude
Code and GitHub Copilot.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.080
Let me bring this all together
with the three things I want you

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:42.510
to walk away with today.

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:46.640
First, trust is built
in, not bolted on.

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:49.250
We started this talk by
saying the hard part

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:52.550
of AI is not building it,
it's shipping it safely,

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:54.890
and Purview supports
that equation.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:56.030
Governance, security,

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:59.280
and compliance are not things
you wire up after the fact

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:02.640
or scramble to add
before a launch review.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.830
They are embedded in
your development stack.

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:08.520
That means every app, every
agent, every workflow starts

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.880
with trust as the default,
not the exception.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:15.060
Second, one policy
layer everywhere.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:17.940
We showed you how Purview
works natively in Foundry,

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:22.080
how it extends to custom apps
and agents through the SDK,

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.240
and how it now covers
local agents running

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:26.880
on developer machines
and endpoints.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.250
The key insight is that you
don't need different governance

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:32.430
strategies for different
deployment models.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:35.400
One framework gives you
consistent visibility protection

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:39.330
and risk control no matter
where your AI lives.

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:43.890
Third, this gets you from
prototype to production faster.

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:45.930
Remember those friction
points we talked

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:48.630
about at the beginning:
compliance delays,

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:49.560
governance overhead,

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:53.280
data exposure risks,
missing audit trails?

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:57.210
Purview addresses all of these
with automated classification,

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:01.050
built-in DLP, and continuous
compliance monitoring.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:04.020
The result is that trust
becomes an accelerator,

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.670
not a bottleneck.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:08.520
Your team spends less
time navigating approvals

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:10.860
and more time shipping great AI.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:15.060
With that, here's my
ask: Please go try it.

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:16.830
If you're already
building in Foundry,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.560
Purview is right there
waiting for you.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:21.960
Just start exploring the
Governance controls we demoed

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:26.130
today, and if you're building
custom apps or agents or working

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:29.460
with third-party
frameworks, go grab the SDK

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:30.840
and start integrating.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.960
Either way, Purview is ready
to be your trust layer,

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:36.570
and let's ship AI that
your organization

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:39.510
and your customers can rely on.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.880
I'll leave you here
with a few resources

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.700
for you to go try hands-on.

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:48.010
With that, thank you
for your attention.
